# 1D Advection and Burgers Equations

## Overview

This project studies the numerical solution of the one-dimensional linear advection equation and the nonlinear Burgers equation using explicit finite-difference methods in MATLAB.

The main objective is to investigate numerical stability, dissipation, dispersion and nonlinear wave behaviour under different discretization parameters.

This work was completed individually as part of the undergraduate course **Computational Fluid Dynamics** at the University of Patras, Department of Mechanical Engineering and Aeronautics.

---

## Governing Equations

### Linear Advection Equation

\[
\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}
+
a\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}
=0
\]

The analytical solution corresponds to the translation of the initial condition:

\[
u(x,t)=u_0(x-at)
\]

and is used as a reference for evaluating the numerical schemes.

### Burgers Equation

The inviscid and viscous forms considered are:

\[
\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}
+
u\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}
=0
\]

and

\[
\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}
+
u\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}
=
\nu \frac{\partial^2u}{\partial x^2}
\]

The inviscid formulation was also examined in conservative form:

\[
\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}
+
\frac{\partial}{\partial x}
\left(
\frac{u^2}{2}
\right)
=0
\]

---

## Numerical Methods

The linear advection equation is solved using three explicit schemes:

- FTBS
- Lax
- Lax–Wendroff

The implementation includes:

- CFL-based time-step calculation
- automatic adjustment of the final time step
- analytical-reference solution
- treatment of propagation direction
- vectorized MATLAB implementation

For Burgers equation, the numerical formulation also includes adaptive time stepping based on the local maximum solution value and an additional diffusion-based stability restriction for the viscous case.

---

## Main Studies

### Numerical Dissipation and Dispersion

The three schemes are compared against the analytical solution.

The results illustrate the typical behaviour of each scheme:

- **FTBS:** primarily numerical dissipation
- **Lax:** stronger numerical diffusion
- **Lax–Wendroff:** reduced diffusion but more pronounced dispersive oscillations

The effect of the CFL number on the Lax–Wendroff solution is also investigated.

### Grid Resolution

Coarse-grid cases are used to demonstrate how insufficient spatial resolution affects the accuracy of both first- and second-order schemes.

### Nonlinear Burgers Dynamics

The Burgers equation is used to study nonlinear wave deformation.

The inviscid formulation develops steeper gradients due to the solution-dependent propagation speed, while the viscous formulation produces a smoother profile because of diffusion.

